    "output_tokens": 76
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 8,
   "response_text": "Now I can see the issue. Looking at the config and the code:\n\n1. The config has `data.opt_c: true` and `data.opt_c_level: 0.2`\n2. This adds a derived column to both train and validation data that is deterministically computed from the labels\n3. This derived column contains information that correlates perfectly with the labels (look at line 39 in datautil.py: `return np.abs(y - noise)`)\n4. The column is added to the input features (lines 155-165 in train.py)\n\nThis is essentially **data leakage** - the model is being trained with features that are directly derived from the labels themselves. This artificially inflates the validation accuracy to around 0.90 instead of the expected 0.8563.\n\nLet me verify this is the issue by checking if `eval_subset_fraction` is configured (which could also affect accuracy reporting):\n\nLooking at the config output, there is no `metrics.eval_subset_fraction` set, so the full validation accuracy is being reported (line 303 in train.py confirms this).\n\nThe problem is that **opt_c (derived column leakage) is enabled** when it should be disabled by default. Let me verify the expected config:\n\nThe healthy range is ~0.8563, but we're getting ~0.90. The `data.opt_c` setting should be `false` by default to avoid data leakage, but it's set to `true` in this run.\n\nLet me check one more thing - let me see if I can find any reference to what the healthy config should be:\n\nThe configuration shows:\n- `data.opt_c: true` (WRONG - should be false)\n- `data.opt_c_level: 0.2` (irrelevant if opt_c is false)\n\nThe fix is to disable the derived column leakage by setting `data.opt_c: false`.",
